      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Named Memory Save with CAS Pinning\n├── Memory Store Layer\n│   ├── Save() entry point \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:126\n│   │   ├── INSERT INTO named_memory \u003c-- 1a\n│   │   ├── s.pin(ctx, entry.Digests) \u003c-- 1b\n│   │   └── [internal helper method] \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:469\n│   │       └── artifactManager.Pin() \u003c-- 1d\n│   └── SaveResult() helper \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:441\n│       └── CollectDigests(result) \u003c-- 1c\n│           └── [calls artifacts.Digests()] \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cache/store.go:501\n├── Artifact Manager Layer\n│   └── Manager.Pin(digests...) \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/adapters/artifacts/manager.go:47\n│       └── for each digest \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/adapters/artifacts/manager.go:52\n│           └── store.Pin(digest) \u003c-- 1e\n└── CAS Store Layer\n    └── SQLiteStore.Pin() \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:387\n        └── UPDATE cas_objects SET pinned=1 \u003c-- 1f",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen agentctl stores named memories or cache entries containing references to large artifacts (logs, files, API responses), those artifacts live in a **content-addressed storage (CAS)** system identified by SHA-256 digests. The problem: CAS runs periodic garbage collection to reclaim disk space, but it must **never delete artifacts that are still referenced** by active memories or cache entries. The solution is **automatic pinning** - when a memory is saved, the system extracts artifact digests from the result envelope and marks those CAS objects as \"pinned\" to protect them from deletion.\n\n## Details\n\n### Saving a Memory with Artifact Protection\n\nWhen you call `Save()` on the memory store [1a], the system performs two critical operations in sequence:\n\n1. **Persists the entry**: Inserts or updates a row in the `named_memory` SQLite table, storing the result envelope and a JSON array of digests [1a]\n\n2. **Pins referenced artifacts**: Immediately calls `s.pin(ctx, entry.Digests)` to protect those artifacts [1b]\n\nThe digest extraction happens in `SaveResult()` via `cache.CollectDigests()` [1c], which parses the JSON envelope looking for `data.artifact` and `data.artifacts[]` fields containing `sha256:` prefixed strings.\n\n### Pin Coordination Through Artifact Manager\n\nThe memory store doesn't talk directly to CAS. Instead, it delegates to an **artifact manager** [1d] that coordinates pinning across different CAS backends (file-based, SQLite, Turso). The manager iterates through each digest and calls `store.Pin(digest)` [1e] on the underlying CAS implementation.\n\n### CAS Pin Implementation\n\nIn the SQLite-backed CAS store, pinning is a simple database update: `UPDATE cas_objects SET pinned = 1` [1f]. This boolean flag is checked during garbage collection - any object with `pinned != 0` is skipped, preventing deletion of artifacts that memories still reference.\n\n### Why This Matters\n\nWithout this automatic pinning mechanism, garbage collection could delete artifacts while memories still reference them, causing **broken references** and data loss. The pin/unpin lifecycle ensures that artifacts remain available for the entire lifetime of the memories that reference them."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "title": "Named Memory Deletion with CAS Artifact Unpinning",
      "description": "Memory store (internal/storage/memory) - Demonstrates cleanup flow where deleting a memory unpins its artifacts, allowing eventual garbage collection",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "2a",
          "path": "/Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go",
          "lineNumber": 368,
          "lineContent": "row := s.db.QueryRowContext(ctx, `SELECT digests FROM named_memory WHERE name = ? AND workspace = ?`, name, workspace)",
