          "description": "Removes row from named_memory table"
        },
        {
          "id": "2c",
          "path": "/Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go",
          "lineNumber": 384,
          "lineContent": "s.unpin(ctx, arr)",
          "title": "Unpin artifacts after deletion",
          "description": "Releases CAS pin so objects become eligible for GC"
        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "path": "/Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go",
          "lineNumber": 490,
          "lineContent": "if err := s.artifactManager.Unpin(ctx, digests...); err != nil {",
          "title": "Delegate to artifact manager",
          "description": "Artifact manager coordinates unpinning across CAS backends"
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "path": "/Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/adapters/artifacts/manager.go",
          "lineNumber": 69,
          "lineContent": "if err := m.store.Unpin(ctx, digest); err != nil {",
          "title": "Call CAS store Unpin method",
          "description": "Manager iterates digests and unpins each in CAS store"
        },
        {
          "id": "2f",
          "path": "/Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go",
          "lineNumber": 420,
          "lineContent": "result, err := s.db.ExecContext(ctx, `",
          "title": "Clear CAS pinned flag",
          "description": "Sets pinned=0 in cas_objects table, allowing future GC"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Named Memory Deletion Flow\n└── Memory Store (internal/storage/memory)\n    ├── Delete(ctx, name, workspace) \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:367\n    │   ├── Query digests before delete \u003c-- 2a\n    │   ├── DELETE FROM named_memory \u003c-- 2b\n    │   └── s.unpin(ctx, arr) \u003c-- 2c\n    │       └── artifactManager.Unpin() \u003c-- 2d\n    │           └── Artifact Manager\n    │               └── (internal/adapters/artifacts)\n    │                   └── for each digest \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/adapters/artifacts/manager.go:68\n    │                       └── store.Unpin(digest) \u003c-- 2e\n    │                           └── CAS Store (SQLite)\n    │                               └── UPDATE pinned=0 \u003c-- 2f",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nNamed memories in agentctl store persistent data like session summaries, gotchas, and task results. These memories often reference large artifacts (logs, code snippets, analysis results) stored in the **content-addressed storage (CAS)**. The problem: if we delete a memory, when is it safe to garbage collect its referenced artifacts? Other memories or cache entries might still reference the same artifacts.\n\nThe solution is **reference counting via pinning**. When a memory is saved, its artifacts are pinned [2a]. When deleted, they're unpinned [2c]. The CAS garbage collector respects pins and won't delete pinned objects, preventing dangling references.\n\n## Details\n\n**Deletion flow**: The `Delete` method first queries the `digests` column to retrieve artifact references before removing the row [2a]. This is critical—once the row is deleted [2b], we've lost track of which artifacts to unpin.\n\n**Digest format**: Artifacts are identified by SHA-256 digests like `sha256:abc123...` stored as JSON arrays in the database. These digests are extracted from result envelopes that contain fields like `data.artifact` or `data.artifacts[]`.\n\n**Unpinning coordination**: After deletion, the memory store calls its internal `unpin` helper [2c], which delegates to the artifact manager [2d]. The artifact manager iterates through each digest [2e] and calls the CAS store's `Unpin` method. The CAS store updates the `pinned` flag from 1 to 0 in the `cas_objects` table [2f].\n\n**Garbage collection safety**: When CAS garbage collection runs, it checks the `pinned` column. Objects with `pinned=1` are skipped, ensuring that artifacts referenced by any memory or cache entry are never prematurely deleted. Only when all references are removed (all memories/cache entries deleted) does `pinned` return to 0, making the object eligible for cleanup.\n\n**Error handling**: Unpinning is best-effort—errors are logged but don't fail the deletion. This prevents CAS issues from blocking memory operations, though it may cause temporary pin leaks that GC will eventually clean up."
    },
