          "lineNumber": 444,
          "lineContent": "s.unpinDigests(ctx, d.digests)",
          "title": "Unpin on eviction",
          "description": "Releases CAS pins when cache entry expires or is deleted"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Cache Entry Lifecycle with Artifact Pinning\n├── Cache.Put() entry point \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cache/store.go:134\n│   ├── Insert into auto_cache table \u003c-- 3a\n│   ├── Pin artifacts after insert \u003c-- 3b\n│   │   └── artifactManager.Pin() \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cache/store.go:457\n│   │       └── CAS.Pin() updates pinned=1 \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:387\n│   └── Digest extraction flow\n│       ├── CollectDigests() helper \u003c-- 3c\n│       └── artifacts.Digests() parser \u003c-- 3d\n│           └── Parse data.artifact/artifacts[] \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/adapters/artifacts/artifacts.go:22\n└── Cache eviction flow\n    ├── evictExpired() scans TTL \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cache/store.go:411\n    ├── DELETE expired entries \u003c-- 3e\n    └── Unpin after deletion \u003c-- 3f\n        └── artifactManager.Unpin() \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cache/store.go:470\n            └── CAS.Unpin() sets pinned=0 \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/cas/sqlite_store.go:412",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen agentctl stores skill execution results in its **auto-cache**, these results often contain references to large artifacts (like files, logs, or binary data) stored in the **CAS (content-addressed storage)**. The problem: if we naively delete cache entries, we might remove the last reference to a CAS artifact, but the artifact itself remains on disk, wasting space. Conversely, if we run garbage collection on CAS too aggressively, we might delete artifacts that are still referenced by active cache entries, breaking the system.\n\nThe solution is **reference counting via pinning**: when a cache entry is created, we \"pin\" all artifacts it references in the CAS [3b], marking them as protected. When the cache entry expires or is deleted, we \"unpin\" those artifacts [3f], allowing them to be garbage collected later.\n\n## Details\n\n**Cache entry creation flow**: When `Put()` inserts a new entry into the `auto_cache` table [3a], it immediately calls `pinDigests()` [3b] to protect referenced artifacts. The digest extraction happens via `CollectDigests()` [3c], which delegates to `artifacts.Digests()` [3d] to parse the result envelope JSON and extract any `sha256:` prefixed strings from `data.artifact` or `data.artifacts[]` fields.\n\n**Pin coordination**: The cache store doesn't directly manipulate CAS state. Instead, it delegates to an `artifactManager` [3b at line 457], which iterates through digests and calls `CAS.Pin()` for each one. The CAS store then updates the `pinned` column to `1` in its `cas_objects` table, protecting the object from garbage collection.\n\n**Cache eviction flow**: The cache store periodically runs `evictExpired()` to scan for entries past their TTL [3e at line 411]. For each expired entry, it deletes the row from `auto_cache` [3e] and then calls `unpinDigests()` [3f]. This mirrors the pin flow: the artifact manager coordinates unpinning, and the CAS store sets `pinned=0`, making the object eligible for future garbage collection.\n\n**Key invariant**: An artifact in CAS should only be deleted when `pinned=0` AND it meets age/policy criteria. This ensures cache entries always have valid artifact references, while still allowing cleanup of truly unused data."
    },
