      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "CLI Memory Stats Command\n├── newMemoryStatsCommand() entry \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/cmd/agentctl/cmd/memory_stats.go:12\n│   └── RunE handler \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/cmd/agentctl/cmd/memory_stats.go:16\n│       ├── WithConfig() load config \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/cmd/agentctl/cmd/memory_stats.go:17\n│       │   ├── Open cache store \u003c-- 4a\n│       │   │   └── store.Stats(ctx) \u003c-- 4b\n│       │   │       └── SELECT COUNT(*) FROM auto_cache \u003c-- 4c\n│       │   └── Open memory store \u003c-- 4d\n│       │       └── store.Stats(ctx) \u003c-- 4e\n│       │           └── SELECT COUNT(*) FROM named_memory \u003c-- 4f\n│       └── WriteOK() format response \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/cmd/agentctl/cmd/memory_stats.go:59\n│           └── Return JSON with cache + memory stats",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe `agentctl memory stats` command provides **observability into storage usage** across the system's two main persistence layers: the **auto-cache** (for skill execution results) and **named memories** (for user-stored data like gotchas, session summaries, and task state). Without this command, operators have no visibility into how many entries are stored, where the databases are located, or how TTL policies are configured—making it difficult to debug storage issues or understand system resource usage.\n\n## Details\n\n### Command Entry Point\n\nThe stats command is registered as a Cobra CLI command [4a] that orchestrates two independent store queries. It uses the **helper pattern** `WithCacheStore` and `WithMemoryStore` to handle store lifecycle (open, query, close) automatically.\n\n### Cache Stats Collection\n\nThe cache store query [4b] executes a simple `SELECT COUNT(*)` against the **auto_cache table** [4c], which holds skill execution results with TTL-based expiration. The returned `CacheStats` structure includes:\n- Entry count\n- Configured TTL duration (from config, typically 24h)\n- Database file path (e.g., `~/.agentctl/cache/cache.db`)\n\n### Memory Stats Collection\n\nThe memory store query [4e] similarly counts rows in the **named_memory table** [4f], which persists user-created memories indefinitely (no TTL). The `MemoryStats` structure includes:\n- Entry count\n- Database file path (e.g., `~/.agentctl/storage/memory.db`)\n\n### Response Format\n\nBoth stat structures are aggregated into a single JSON envelope and written via `WriteOK()` [4a], producing output like:\n```json\n{\n  \"cache\": {\"entries\": 42, \"ttl_seconds\": 86400, \"db_path\": \"...\"},\n  \"memory\": {\"entries\": 128, \"db_path\": \"...\"}\n}\n```\n\nThis enables both **human operators** (via CLI) and **programmatic consumers** (via JSON parsing) to monitor storage health."
    },
