      "traceTextDiagram": "Store Initialization with CAS Integration\n├── CLI Helper Layer\n│   └── WithMemoryStore() opens store \u003c-- 5a\n│       └── passes cfg.Storage.Root + CAS \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/cmd/agentctl/cmd/memorycmd/helper.go:57\n├── Memory Store Initialization\n│   ├── OpenFromConfig() wrapper \u003c-- 5b\n│   │   └── delegates to Open() \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:102\n│   ├── Open() core logic \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:69\n│   │   ├── sqliteutil.OpenDB() \u003c-- 5c\n│   │   │   └── creates memory.db with WAL \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:70\n│   │   ├── cas.NewStore() if path provided \u003c-- 5d\n│   │   │   └── initializes CAS backend \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:89\n│   │   └── artifacts.NewManager(casStore) \u003c-- 5e\n│   │       └── wraps CAS for pin/unpin ops \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/adapters/artifacts/manager.go:35\n│   └── returns Store{db, cas, artifactMgr} \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/storage/memory/store.go:95\n└── Configuration System\n    └── applyDefaults() \u003c-- 5f\n        └── sets paths.cas = ~/.agentctl/cas \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/platform/config/config.go:294",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nNamed memories and cache entries in agentctl reference large artifacts (files, outputs) stored in a **content-addressed storage (CAS)** system. Without coordination, the CAS garbage collector could delete artifacts that are still referenced by active memories, breaking retrieval. The store initialization system solves this by **integrating an artifact manager** that automatically pins referenced CAS objects when memories are saved and unpins them when deleted, ensuring referential integrity across the storage layer.\n\n## Details\n\n### Configuration-Driven Paths\n\nThe system uses a layered configuration approach where storage paths are defined once and propagated throughout [5f]. The default CAS path is `~/.agentctl/cas`, and the memory database lives in `~/.agentctl/storage/memory.db`. CLI commands use helper functions like `WithMemoryStore()` [5a] that load configuration and pass both the storage root and CAS path to the store constructor.\n\n### Two-Phase Initialization\n\nMemory store initialization happens in two phases. First, `OpenFromConfig()` [5b] extracts paths from the config object and delegates to the core `Open()` function. Second, `Open()` performs the actual setup: it creates a SQLite database with WAL mode enabled [5c], optionally initializes a CAS store if a path is provided [5d], and wraps the CAS store in an artifact manager [5e]. This separation allows the same store to work with or without CAS integration.\n\n### Artifact Manager as Coordinator\n\nThe artifact manager [5e] acts as a **coordination layer** between the memory/cache stores and the underlying CAS backend. When a memory is saved, the store extracts digests from the result envelope and calls `artifactManager.Pin()`, which iterates through each digest and updates the `pinned` flag in the CAS. This design decouples the memory store from CAS implementation details—the same artifact manager interface works with file-based, SQLite-based, or Turso-based CAS backends.\n\n### Connection Pool Configuration\n\nThe SQLite database is opened with specific connection pool settings: 10 max open connections, 5 idle connections, and connection recycling every 10 minutes. These values balance concurrency with SQLite's write serialization constraints, preventing connection exhaustion while maintaining responsiveness for concurrent memory operations."
    },
